# ktlint configuration (see the ktlint plugin in build.gradle.kts).
#
# ONE rule is on: imports nothing references. Everything else stays off on
# purpose. This tree predates the formatter by thousands of lines, and turning
# the rest on would either fail the check on day one or invite a whole-tree
# reformat that no one could review - neither of which is why this exists.
#
# What it protects: dead imports are the one kind of junk that accumulates
# silently, because the compiler accepts them and nothing else looks. The
# whole set was swept out by hand once (the sweep that produced this file);
# this is what stops the next few hundred arriving.
#
# The android ruleset is disabled too: the ktlint plugin enables it for
# Android projects, and its rules are not the one this check is for.
# Conventions the checker does not enforce, stated here so they are written
# down rather than folklore:
#
#   indent_style = space, indent_size = 4
#       Four spaces is what the tree uses. Two files drifted to two spaces
#       before this was recorded; if ktlint's indent rule is ever enabled, this
#       is the setting it should be driven by.
#
#   max_line_length = off (deliberate)
#       The tree is KDoc-heavy and the long lines are almost all prose that
#       reads worse wrapped. There is no mechanical cap; keep new code under
#       ~120 columns where it does not hurt readability.
#
#   imports: ASCII-sorted, one symbol per line, no wildcards
#       `ktlint_standard` is disabled, so nothing checks this today.
#
#   one symbol per FQN: import it, do not spell it out in a code body
#       A fully-qualified name inline (com.kennyb1201.kbstream.…) is reserved
#       for a genuine name clash, with a comment saying why.
#
#   TAG per class: `private const val TAG = "<MODULE>"`
#       No inline string literals in Log calls, and TAG is private unless a
#       second file really does reuse it.
#
#   error handling: pick the construct by what the failure MEANS
#       - runCatchingCancellable  cancellable coroutine work (rethrows
#                                 CancellationException; plain runCatching
#                                 swallows it and breaks structured concurrency)
#       - runCatching             a non-cancellable failure you intend to absorb
#       - try/catch               multi-catch, a finally, or a rethrow
#       Do not mix runCatching and try in the same file without a reason.
#
#   failure reporting: Log.w for expected/user-recoverable, CrashReporter for
#       unexpected/programmer error. A defensive catch that swallows a bug
#       (an empty `catch (_: Exception) {}`) is the case CrashReporter exists
#       for - it is invisible on a TV otherwise. CrashReporter.recordEvent is
#       for expected churn (a dead stream) that should be named in the
#       diagnostics export but NOT sent to Sentry.
#
#   JSON: Moshi for network/API models, kotlinx.serialization for sync
#       payloads and schema-shaped data. Do not add the other to a file
#       without a comment saying why.
[*.{kt,kts}]
indent_style = space
indent_size = 4
ktlint_standard = disabled
ktlint_android = disabled
ktlint_standard_no-unused-imports = enabled
